\par Commercial Use of PHP with SQL Backends:
\par Application Development with PHP
\par \pard\plain \s1\qr 
\par presented by Jeffrey Buchbinder (jeff@univrel.pr.uconn.edu)
\par at ApacheCon 2000 (Orlando, FL)
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par 
\par \page\pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b I.}\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b\ls0 Determining the Objective
\par \pard\plain \s1\b0 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b Should I use PHP+SQL?
\par \pard\plain \s1\b0 
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\b0\ls6{\ul What advantages does this combination have over ASP or Perl solutions?} -- ASP and Perl are both slower than PHP, which is preprocessed. Also, PHP was developed with database access in mind, whereas ASP was not.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls7{\ul What is the learning curve of PHP and SQL?} -- If you are familiar with Perl, C, or Java, PHP will be a piece of cake for you. SQL is pretty easy to learn, and there are many books out that can help you understand SQL syntax and form.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls8{\b0{\ul What is good preparation for this type of project?} -- Reviewing the PHP manual and tutorial would be advisable, to give you an idea of the constructs and syntax behind the language. Also, perusing the PHP code exchange }(http://px.skylar.com/) {\b0 or PHPwizard 
(}http://www.phpwizard.net/{\b0 ) would be a good idea. You might also want to consider looking for a simple SQL tutorial to get you started.}
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls19{\b0{\ul Have any commerical-grade applications been developed with PHP and SQL?} -- Well, that depends what you mean by commercial-grade. There are many applications that function as well, if not better than their commercial counterparts. There are PHP+SQL applicat
ions everywhere, but the best place to check is }http://www.php.net/projects.php3{\b0  where an enormous list of projects is kept. There are e-commerce applications, bug trackers, email applications, and even a medical practice management suite (which I happen t
o be the head coder of).}
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b Benefits of standard HTML over Javascript/ActiveX/etc
\par 
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls2{\ul One set of code runs everywhere} -- no special cases for browsers. This is especially important in the multiplatform environments that most computer professionals have to deal with.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls3{\ul Stability issues} -- especially in technologies like Javascript. Netscape has been infamous for crashing during execution of Java applets, and Javascript has also been very unstable.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls4{\ul Usually lower loading times, especially compared to Java or ActiveX applets}. -- This is very important when dealing with application development, since most users expect much faster page refreshes to attempt to compensate for the page-oriented nature of HT
ML.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls5{\b0{\ul Security issues with ActiveX} -- ActiveX technology is {\b not} platform independent, and also has security issues stemming from the somewhat uncontrollable nature of its system access.}
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b Disadvantages of standard HTML
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls9{\ul Refreshes to the server have to be done to simulate some widget actions} -- Select boxes and other HTML widgets are more or less static, with no ability apart from Javascript-based kludges to allow their content to change. This is very difficult to design a
round, as most applications have the ability to change data in fields.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls10{\ul Less flash and eye candy / could possibly be more difficult for some users} -- Many people are used to Windows and Macintosh type applications with menu bars, et cetera. Whereas these can be approximated with some degree of difficulty, it is not perfect, an
d users could have problems adapting to the interface.
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1{\b Is this project suitable for web-based implementation?}
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls11{\ul Does it require special widgets that are not HTML?} -- There are color selection widgets and other such tools which are impractical to implement using only HTML. Applications like graphic design and layout are best left to either Java-based solutions or pla
tform dependant solutions, as HTML is not suited for them
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls12{\ul Does it heavily rely on real-time feedback? (client/server push)} -- Whereas real-time client/server push can be implemented through the use of META tags, it is very impractical. Notification routines, et cetera, also become moderately unpractical.
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b What database backend(s) do I want to use?
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls18{\ul Which SQL backend is best?} -- Some swear by PostgreSQL, some by Informix, some by Oracle. I personally prefer MySQL, as it seems to core dump far less than PostgreSQL, which it seems to always be pitted against, but the decision is yours, based on licensin
g, availability, SQL capability, and many other factors.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls13{\ul Will I need an abstraction layer to handle multiple SQL engines?} -- If you are planning on allowing the use of more than one SQL engine (for example, allowing either MySQL or PostgreSQL to be used), then you have to create an SQL abstraction layer to handl
e SQL calls, which should be placed in your API.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls14{\ul Will I need common table definitions that work on multiple engines?} -- There are many dissimilarities between the different SQL engines, including their syntax.
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b What licensing will I want to use?
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls17{\ul GPL/LGPL and derivatives} -- Out of all of the available "open-source" licenses, I would recommend the GPL (GNU Public License) or one of its derivatives. These seem to be the best for community development without the fear of code being used by proprietary
 competitors.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls16{\ul BSD-type licenses} -- This is a "do as you will" license which states that the code is available for any purpose. This, I have found, is not the best way to keep developers, since they can simply pull code from your project and use it in either their own pr
oject or in a commercial competitor.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls15{\ul Other licenses} -- There are far too many "open-source" licenses to discuss here, as well as many closed source licenses, which are impractical due to PHP's script-type syntax.
\par \page\pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b 2.}\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b\ls1 Determining the Implementation (Planning)
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b Benefits of modular design
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls26{\ul Reuse of code allows changes to be propagated across the entire codebase} -- This is very important once your code base grows beyond a few thousand lines of code. Retyping code is a waste of time, and requires changes to be hand-made to the entire project, 
instead of a singular point of control.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls25{\ul What modules will you have?} -- Deciding on what functions should be apportioned to what module should be done by examining the functionality of the application you are porting or the specifications of the application you are designing, and laying out what 
functions should be grouped together, and what functions need to be implemented as modules.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls24{\ul How granular will your divisions be?} -- Designing a modular application is very useful, but you need to decide how much of the application will be contained in each module, and how you will pass control from one module to another.
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b User access levels/accounts
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls28{\ul One SQL account affords more security and control than many} -- With a single real SQL server account, users do not have access to the SQL server directly, and therefore your control over access to data contained therein increases dramatically.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls27{\ul Internal levels: how many do you need?} -- Most applications can get away with standard users and admin users, but some applications need more granular control over user access. This can be accomplished with either a hash or an ancillary database to keep tr
ack of user permissions. 
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b SQL table configuration
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls29{\ul What will give you maximum flexibility with minimum bloat?} -- Try to avoid useless fields, as they become an increasing burden on the PHP
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls30{\ul How will the databases interrelate? How will you link them?} -- I usually use either PostgreSQL OIDs or MySQL AUTO_INCREMENT fields to keep track of a number for each record, then use an INT UNSIGNED field to link that. There are other ways of doing this, h
owever, based on your intended application. To split into multiple tables or not is always a very poignant question. Be sure to have a decent database model before even attempting to code.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls31{\ul Will you depend on PHP or SQL for most of the scut work?} -- SQL can also have internal functions declared. In general, it is best to rely on the SQL server for most of the heavy-duty calculations and intensive operations, but that is not always the case.
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1{\b Libraries and Functions}
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls20{\ul Do you need to write your own?} -- Whenever you find yourself repeating a section of code, write a function for it. Functions are an incredible time-saver, and allow your application to be extended by other developers.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls23{\ul Are there internal PHP functions that will do what you need?} -- Reviewing the online PHP manual, there are many internal functions that work for many important parts of an application. Don't reinvent the wheel.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls22{\ul Do you need exec/shell calls?} -- Try to avoid these at all costs, as they use up valuable tokens. If you can, use internal or user-defined functions.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls21{\ul Do you need to write wrappers for error checking/handling?} -- Some internal PHP functions don't do enough error checking, or do it in a way that would be inconsistant with your API.
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\b Variables and Constants
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls38{\ul Where can you use constants?} -- Constants can be useful for debug statuses, and can be used as one would use C/C++ macros. This becomes exceedingly useful in reducing the number of variables used.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls36{\ul Scope issues (global/local/class)} -- Remember than global variables are not available inside a function or class method without the use of the global keyword, just as class variables are only accessable using the $this keyword. This becomes crucial, as man
y bugs evolve from these mistakes.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls34{\ul Unsetting variables} -- Clean up after yourself. Use the unset() function in PHP to unset your variables, clearing space on the tokens list for other variables. This is very important mainly when dealing with global variables.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls35{\b0\ul Preserving space in the token cache} -- Use associative arrays whenever possible to reduce the number of variables present, and to make passing and utilization of them much easier and more economical.
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\f1\fs18 \bullet }\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls32{\b0\ul Persistant universal variables across the package (this_id, etc)} -- Remember to pass  certain variables both in forms as HIDDEN input types and as parts of URLs when using A HREF tags.
\par \page\pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b 3.}\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b\ls1 Designing the Project
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Naming conventions and the global stack
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par You probably want to keep the number of global variables that are used by the program down, as it slows down execution time, and can provide problems when importing global variables into local functions. If pieces of information have to be passed, try to u
se associative arrays whenever possible. This is, of course, very unhelpful when dealing with loop counters and other such things, but is very useful when trying to keep data together.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example (associative array):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <?php
\par \tab $cost[outgoing_funds] = abs ( 100 - $c );
\par \tab $cost[total_calculated] = $a + $b;
\par \tab $cost[net] = $cost[total_calculated] - $cost[outgoing_funds];
\par ?>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Global variables file
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par A global variables file should have all of the reusable variables that the package needs to function. Examples of variables that should be put here are:
\par \tab Database access information (host, username, password)
\par \tab Package-specific information (name, version, coder)
\par \tab Macros (font tag macros, like $_ft expanding to <FONT FACE="ft">...)
\par 
\par It is a good idea to include a variable based on the name of the file, or an associative array, to let the rest of the modules know that it has been loaded. Such an example would be: $module_loaded[config] = true; This will prove useful in the section of t
he modules where the file has to be loaded.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example (configuration module loader):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <?php
\par \tab // configuration loader section
\par \tab if (!$module_loaded[config]) require "config.php";
\par ?>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par It can also be advantageous to include the loader for the API (discussed next) in this file, as it will reduce the number of includes/requires and checking to see if they are already loaded.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul API (Application Programming Interface)
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par An API allows you to get the maximum effectiveness out of your code, by making reusable segments of code available through functions to every module in your package. The use of class "containers" to hold data and methods to relate variables to program exec
ution is also recommended when creating an API. Remember that functions should be as universal as possible, and should generally be prefixed by something related to your project (like freemed_display_insco for the freemed project, etc) to avoid conflicts b
etween custom designed modules, APIs, and built in PHP functions.
\par It can be quite advantageous to call the API from the configuration file to keep the number of includes/requires down, and to simplify the amount of repeated code appearing at the top of each module.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul The Modular Philosophy
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par Even though a project can be coded in a single PHP file, it is often advantageous to split code into smaller modules, which can be separately maintained. It is, however, a bad idea to take this philosophy too far and split every action into a separate PHP 
script. I have used a variable that I call $action to pass the intended idea from one place to another, allowing similar program functions to stay together.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example ($action switch for module):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <?php
\par switch ($action) \{
\par \tab case "addform":
\par \tab // code for addition form here
\par \tab break;
\par 
\par \tab case "add":
\par \tab // code for actual addition here
\par \tab break;
\par 
\par \tab default:
\par \tab // where we go if none of the above
\par \tab break;
\par \} // end master action switch\line ?>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par You may wish to be more granular than I have been in this example. It is also useful sometimes to combine like actions using internal switching to allow common use of code (example: addition and modification forms). The combination of different actions ont
o a single page allows for selectable action from a pulldown, with no need for Javascript, since the FORM ACTION="page" tag does not readily allow for changes in the manner that would be required.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Working in More than Two Dimensions
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par Normally, we are limited by SQL to working in two dimensions, because of the limitation of SQL tables. There is a way to "squash" more information into an SQL table, and in effect create a three-dimensional table to store data. This is accomplished by "squ
ashing" using PHP's join and explode commands. With an error-checking wrapper, this becomes very easy.
\par \page\pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b 4.}\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ul\b\ls1 Coding in PHP/SQL
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Tips and Tricks: Arrays
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par Passing arrays is essential for many database functions, and this becomes even more crucial if working with three-dimensional arrays. This can be accomplished by using a set of square brackets, {\b [ ]}, after the variable name, to tell the PHP preprocessor tha
t multiple variables are to be returned. These are then handled like an array.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example (passing arrays from HTML):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <FORM ACTION="this_page.php" METHOD=POST>
\par \tab <INPUT TYPE=CHECKBOX NAME="check[]" VALUE="1">1<BR>
\par \tab <INPUT TYPE=CHECKBOX NAME="check[]" VALUE="2">2<BR>
\par \tab <INPUT TYPE=TEXT NAME="text[]" VALUE="1"><BR>
\par \tab <INPUT TYPE=TEXT NAME="text[]" VALUE="2"><BR>
\par \tab <INPUT TYPE=HIDDEN NAME="hidden[]" VALUE="hidden1">
\par \tab <INPUT TYPE=HIDDEN NAME="hidden[]" VALUE="hidden2">
\par </FORM>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par They would be referenced as $check[1], $check[2], $text[1], $text[2], $hidden[1], and $hidden[2].
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Tips and Tricks: Authentication
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par PHP has many types of authentication that are available to it, but the basic HTTP authentication protocol is very easy to implement, and provides an easy way to restrict access.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example (authentication function example):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <?php
\par $package_name = "(your package name)"; // can come from configuration file
\par $admin_users = array(
\par \tab "admin"=>"password",
\par \tab "root"=>"password"
\par \tab ); // declare associative array of admin users and passwords
\par 
\par function authenticate ($null_value="") \{
\par global $admin_users, $package_name, $PHP_AUTH_USER, $PHP_AUTH_PW;
\par if (!isset($PHP_AUTH_USER)) \{
\par \tab Header("WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm=\\"$package_name\\"");
\par \tab Header("HTTP/1.0 401 Unauthorized");
\par \tab include "header.php";
\par \tab echo "
\par \tab <B>ACCESS DENIED!</B>
\par \tab ";
\par \tab include "footer.php";
\par \tab die("");
\par \} else \{ // if a username *was* provided
\par \tab if (($admin_users[$PHP_AUTH_USER] != $PHP_AUTH_PW) or
\par \tab (empty($admin_users[$PHP_AUTH_USER]))) \{
\par \tab // if incorrect, try to reauthenticate
\par \tab Header("WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm=\\"$package_name\\"");
\par \tab Header("HTTP/1.0 401 Unauthorized");
\par \tab include "header.php";
\par \tab echo "
\par \tab <B>ACCESS DENIED!</B>
\par \tab ";
\par \tab include "footer.php";
\par \tab die("");
\par \tab \}// end of attempt to reauthenticate
\par  \} // end checking if string provided
\par 
\par // code from the PhotoSeek project (http://photoseek.sourceforge.net)
\par ?>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Tips and Tricks: Scope
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par Scope in PHP does not work the same way as scope in C or Perl works. Every variable is local to the function it is in, and a variable has to be declared global to be passed from the global variable stack. In classes, the "$this" variable, much like in Java
/Javascript, is used to pass variables local to the class into its methods.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example (scope and global variables):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <?php
\par $g = "global variable";
\par 
\par function foo_bar ($optional_parameter = "") \{
\par \tab global $g; // imports variable g
\par `\tab return substr ($g, 0, 1);
\par \} // end function foo_bar
\par ?>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain \s1\ul Tips and Tricks: The Trinary Operator and Select Statements
\par \pard\plain \s1\ulnone 
\par \pard\plain \s1 The moderately undocumented "trinary operator" allows for embedded if/then/else clauses to be used in string generation. This allows for many code shortcuts, and has a particularly good use in generating SELECT form widgets with preselected values.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat4 Example (trinary operator and select statements):
\par \pard\plain \s1\cbpat5\i <?php
\par \tab // $selected_value is being passed here
\par \tab echo "
\par \tab \tab <SELECT NAME=\\"selected_value\\">
\par \tab \tab <OPTION VALUE=\\"1\\" ".
\par \tab \tab ( ($selected_value==1) ? "SELECTED" : "" ) ."1
\par \tab \tab <OPTION VALUE=\\"2\\" ".
\par \tab \tab ( ($selected_value==2) ? "SELECTED" : "" ) ."2
\par \tab \tab </SELECT>
\par \tab ";
\par ?>
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par \page\pard\plain \s1 
\par \pard\plain {\listtext\pard\plain \li283\ri0\fi-283 5.}\ilvl0 \s1\li283\ri0\fi-283\ls1{\b\ul Bughunting and Testing}
\par \pard\plain \s1 
\par The importance of {\b Commenting}
\par \tab Uncommented code is impossible to debug, or at least, very difficult
\par \tab Commenting is amazingly useful for braces \{ \} and other constructs
\par 
\par The importance of {\b indentation} and {\b consistency}
\par \tab Indenting and breaking up lines helps readability
\par \tab Using the same code style also helps readability
\par 
\par The universal $debug or $VB variable or constant
\par \tab Universally set debug on or off from centralized location
\par \tab Don't forget to declare it global in internal functions
\par 
\par Separation of SQL queries into formation and execution
\par \tab By first forming the query in a string and then executing, errors can be caught
\par 
\par Use of PHPs source view to pinpoint problems
\par \tab Linking (filename).phps to (filename).php3 lets you see highlighted source
\par 
\par Use of PHP's internal variables to report errors
\par \tab __FILE__ and __LINE__ constants can be used to help pinpoint bugs
\par 
\par Partially coding a project makes it easier to debug as you go
\par \tab Start with a bare framework, with API, configuration and some functions
\par \tab Add functionality by module, and test as you go
\par }
